<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/inventions?focus=neolithic-revolution">Inventions &amp; Discoveries</a> · Farming and food</p><h1 class="enc-title">Neolithic Revolution</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as First Agricultural Revolution</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neolithic">Neolithic</a> Revolution, or First Agricultural Revolution, was the transition of many human cultures from hunting and gathering to farming and settlement, which began in the Fertile Crescent between 10,000 and 8000 BCE. Farming also arose separately in other regions, among them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-guinea">New Guinea</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesoamerica">Mesoamerica</a> and three areas of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a>. Surplus food supported denser settlements and specialised labour, and provided the basis for centralised administrations and writing. Diets narrowed, however, and studies find that people grew shorter after the transition.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/neolithic-revolution.jpg" alt="Neolithic Revolution" width="320" height="444" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Iris Groman-Yaroslavski , Ehud Weiss, Dani Nadel · CC BY 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Composite_Sickles_for_Cereal_Harvesting_at_23,000-Years-Old_Ohalo_II,_Israel.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="inv-entry"><h2 class="enc-section-title">On the inventions tree</h2><dl class="inv-entry-facts"><div><dt>First known</dt><dd>c. 10,000 BCE</dd></div><div><dt>Where</dt><dd>The Fertile Crescent</dd></div></dl><p class="inv-entry-note"><span class="inv-k">Also arose independently</span>the Yangtze, China (13,500–8,200 years ago); northern China (c. 6000 BCE); New Guinea (perhaps 8000 BCE); Mesoamerica (c. 7000 BCE); eastern North America (c. 2500 BCE); Ethiopia, the Sahel and West Africa</p><p class="inv-entry-note"><span class="inv-k">Note</span>Dates for its beginning in West Asia range from about 12,000 to 8000 BCE.</p><div class="inv-entry-rel"><span class="inv-k">Built on</span><a class="inv-chip" href="/encyclopedia/stone-tools">Stone tools<span>3.3 million years ago</span></a></div><div class="inv-entry-rel"><span class="inv-k">Led to</span><a class="inv-chip" href="/encyclopedia/mudbrick">Mudbrick<span>c. 9000 BCE</span></a><a class="inv-chip" href="/encyclopedia/clay-tokens">Clay tokens<span>c. 8000 BCE</span></a><a class="inv-chip" href="/encyclopedia/maize">Maize<span>c. 7000 BCE</span></a><a class="inv-chip" href="/encyclopedia/rice">Rice<span>c. 7000 BCE</span></a><a class="inv-chip" href="/encyclopedia/potato">Potato<span>8000–5000 BCE</span></a><a class="inv-chip" href="/encyclopedia/irrigation">Irrigation<span>c. 6000 BCE</span></a><a class="inv-chip" href="/encyclopedia/cotton">Cotton<span>c. 6000 BCE</span></a><a class="inv-chip" href="/encyclopedia/sericulture">Silk<span>c. 3500 BCE</span></a><a class="inv-chip" href="/encyclopedia/plough">Plough<span>c. 2800 BCE</span></a><a class="inv-chip" href="/encyclopedia/tea">Tea<span>2nd century BCE</span></a><a class="inv-chip" href="/encyclopedia/sugarcane">Sugar<span>c. 350 CE</span></a><a class="inv-chip" href="/encyclopedia/green-revolution">Green Revolution<span>1940s–1960s</span></a></div><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/inventions?focus=neolithic-revolution">See it on the inventions tree &rarr;</a></p></section>
